From 90328709b997e99e9c7466669198f2f37fad676e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Chrzanowski <simonch@gmx.de>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 09:33:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fftools/ffmpeg_sched: fix decoder starvation in
 unchoke_downstream()
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

03dfac56 ("fftools/ffmpeg_sched: allow throttling decoder outputs") gated the
downstream traversal on the decoder's own choked_next:

    case SCH_NODE_TYPE_DEC:
        dec = &sch->dec[dst->idx];
        if (!dec->waiter.choked_next) {
            for (int i = 0; i < dec->nb_outputs; i++)
                unchoke_downstream(sch, dec->outputs[i].dst);
        }
        break;

schedule_update_locked() resets every choked_next at the top of the pass and
clears them one stream at a time as it walks the graph, so whether this gate
lets the traversal through depends on whether this particular decoder happened
to be cleared earlier in the same pass. When it blocks, everything downstream
of the decoder stays choked, the decoder's output backs up and it stops
draining its own overflow FIFO.

With two output streams this starves one decoder for most of the run. On a
4742 s MPEG-TS capture transcoded to video+audio, instrumenting the FIFO gives:

    dec0 video  in=234477 parked=234387 served=103314 direct=90  dropped=0
    dec1 audio  in=197582 parked=195399 served=195399 direct=2183 dropped=0

Audio drains completely; video parks 234387 packets and is served 103314. The
missing 131073 are never dropped anywhere — they sit in the FIFO until
teardown, so ffmpeg exits 0, prints no warning and shows no drop= in the stats
line while writing 42% of the frames. The count varies from run to run because
the traversal order does.

Remove the gate. Verified on the affected file (237062 frames, five runs,
previously 96314-110064 varying), on an unaffected capture (207740, unchanged)
and video-only (237062, unchanged). Reverting 03dfac56 wholesale gives the same
237062, so this restores the pre-commit behaviour without giving up the
decoder throttling the commit adds.
---
 fftools/ffmpeg_sched.c | 13 +++++++++----
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/fftools/ffmpeg_sched.c b/fftools/ffmpeg_sched.c
index 8ec3bdaf80..cec60f111c 100644
--- a/fftools/ffmpeg_sched.c
+++ b/fftools/ffmpeg_sched.c
@@ -1338,10 +1338,15 @@ static void unchoke_downstream(Scheduler *sch, SchedulerNode *dst)
     switch (dst->type) {
     case SCH_NODE_TYPE_DEC:
         dec = &sch->dec[dst->idx];
-        if (!dec->waiter.choked_next) {
-            for (int i = 0; i < dec->nb_outputs; i++)
-                unchoke_downstream(sch, dec->outputs[i].dst);
-        }
+        // Do NOT gate this on dec->waiter.choked_next. schedule_update_locked
+        // resets every choked_next at the top of the pass and clears them as it
+        // walks the graph, so gating here makes the traversal depend on the
+        // order streams happen to be visited in. When it blocks, everything
+        // downstream of the decoder stays choked, its output backs up and it
+        // stops draining its own overflow FIFO — measured as 131073 video
+        // packets parked and never served, while audio drained fully.
+        for (int i = 0; i < dec->nb_outputs; i++)
+            unchoke_downstream(sch, dec->outputs[i].dst);
         break;
     case SCH_NODE_TYPE_ENC:
         enc = &sch->enc[dst->idx];
